## Commit, and the push held on a stop (2026-09-26)

The commit stage replaces the `<short hash>` placeholders, lands the gate's summary and its two tables, and checks the footers; then it pushes `main` and fast-forwards the container's branch, and removes the worktrees. Since the post-mortem of 2026-09-29 the per-site list lands at one fixed path, `explorations/compile-ladder/gate/distance-sites.tsv`, overwritten at each landing; the specification 
PDF is built once per batch when its text changed, with no log committed; the worktrees' `tmp/` go with them; the two microGPT programs are started under walk in the background on the landed tree and not awaited, and step 4 starts them only when their log does not exist yet, since a run resumed from its journal runs the commit stage with its plain prompt (`postmortem-2026-09-29/script-review.md`, 
finding 4). A worktree is removed without `--force`, after `default_repository/caches/global.map`, the tracked file a build deletes, is restored: git removes an ignored `tmp/` without the flag, and refuses a worktree that holds uncommitted or untracked work, which the stage then keeps and names for the coordinator (`postmortem-2026-09-29/script-review.md`, finding 9). Since 2026-09-26 it pushes on
ly when no landed rung carries a stop that was met and not lifted (`CLIMB-BATCH-6.md` section 8, item 2). `pushHeldBy` gathers the `stopsMet` entries of each landed rung's last worker report and last skeptic verdict, of the merged-diff review, and, since the post-mortem of 2026-09-29, of the repairs on the merged tree, which no second review reads any more (`postmortem-2026-09-29/script-review.md`
, finding 1). It keeps every entry whose `liftedBy` does not cite `POSITIONS.md`, since only Pavol lifts a stop; a malformed entry is kept too. Any entry left gives the commit stage a role with no push step and no worktree removal. The stage then writes a "Not pushed." paragraph naming the stops into the batch's `RECORD.md` and commits it locally, and the script's result carries `pushHeld` and `he
ldBy`. The coordinator pushes once Pavol has lifted them. Batches 4 and 5 held the push by the landing agent's reading of the intro alone, and batch 5's push went out at about 17:56 past rung S's unlifted stop, because the coordinator did not read its record's line before pushing (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-5/RECORD.md`, "The push is held"). A rung that did not land holds nothing, since none of i
ts source is applied.

Since 2026-10-03 step 1 also writes the landed figures into `FACTS.md`, numbers only, from the tables it lands (climb batch 9's review, finding 6): in "The true distance to the switch-over" every figure of the last landed gate, the lines of `distance.txt` it cites and the batch number in its paths, and in "The checker-count stage's table" the count's total and its path. A sentence the new figures 
make false is left for the coordinator's consolidation and named in the stage's summary. At climb batch 9's landing both entries still said batch 8's 565 and 56, the gather having left them for the gate's figures, until the coordinator wrote 340 and 1 at `10c511702`. Of the two homes the finding names, the commit stage's role or the coordinator's landing step, the review's measure 6 takes the comm
it stage, and so does this change: it is the step that already fills the landed hashes and lands the same tables.

No rung compares the corpus's outputs; the suite's verdicts are the check, and a value that matters is asserted in a test (post-mortem 2026-09-29).

## A review that still blocks after its repair (2026-09-29)

The merged-diff review gets one judge and one repair. Since the post-mortem of 2026-09-29 there is no second review inside a batch: the post-batch review is the second look, and a finding the repair left is its item for the next batch. When the review's repair returns nothing, stops or does not land, the code findings the judge upheld are listed as `review-unrepaired.N` for the next batch and Pavo
l, and they do not hold the push; a stop the repair lists holds it as a rung's does (`postmortem-2026-09-29/script-review.md`, finding 1).

## A repair of tests and records only (2026-09-29)

After a repair on the merged tree, the review's or the gate's, the whole gate runs again only when the repair, or the review's corrections, changed a path the gate reads that is not a test file: under `ProjectFortress/` or `Library/`, or `build.xml` (POSITIONS 2026-09-29, on rerunning the gate after a repair that only added tests; batch N's review repair added six compiler test files, run in the h
arness by the repair itself, and the batch waited on a whole gate for them). Until 2026-10-02 any path outside `explorations/` that is not a test file reran it, the specification's text among them; since then (POSITIONS, "Nothing is built or run twice on the same code.") a path no gate stage reads leaves the first gate's tables standing, and the commit stage's step 1a records the repair's commits 
and those paths as `# repair-ungated` lines in `summary.txt`, so that the summary says its tables are of the tree before them. In climb batch 8 the gate ran a second time after a repair of one sentence of `Specification/appendices/changes.tex`, 24 minutes on the critical path, and its tables came out identical but for timing (`coordinator/build-cache-exploration.md`, section 4; `reviews/batch-8-re
view.md`, section 4). The review's own corrections follow the same rule: a corrected path the gate reads makes the gate beside it stale, as before; one it does not read is recorded beside it. A test file is a `.fss`, `.fsi` or `.test` file directly in `ProjectFortress/tests/` or a `ProjectFortress/*_tests/` directory. The repair returns `headBefore`, `headAfter` and `pathsChanged`, and in `testRun
s` each test file it added or changed, run in the harness on the merged tree with the gate's own JUnit mechanics, the files of one corpus together in one harness run, one JVM (below, "Rules weighed by what they cost", item 6; batch N's repair ran each in its own JVM, `compile-ladder/climb-batch-N/merged-tests/repair-junit-placed.txt`): `ONE_JVM=1 junit.sh` beside that capture for the `.test` files
 of `compiler_tests/`, and again for those of `library_tests/`, `compile-ladder/rung-inference-walk/harness-one.sh` once for the interpreter tests; each file with its suite row, JUnit cases, verdict (that of the run that held it) and capture. `repairRerun` in the script decides. The gate runs again, as before, on a code path, a changed test no passing run exercises, a run that failed, or a repair 
that listed nothing; no second review follows it since the post-mortem of 2026-09-29, and the runs' captures stay under `tmp/gate-batch-<N>/`, not committed. After the gate's repair it also runs again unless every line the gate was red on is answered by a passing run (`failingAnswered`) and no suite count fell. Otherwise the first gate's tables stand, and the commit stage appends the runs to its `
summary.txt` as `# repair-tests` lines, with their total: the next batch's gate counts rise beyond the summary's rows by those cases, and `gate_compare` skips `#` lines. When a later gate does run, the repair's tests are among its counts and no such lines are written. The script's stubbed check runs both kinds of repair through it: `compile-ladder/plan-n/manifest/checkn.js`, eight merged-tree scen
arios, until the cleaning removed it from the tree at `ab067d9b6`; since 2026-09-30, `coordinator/tools/workflow-scenarios.js`, scenarios R5, A7 to A9 and A14 ("Measured, and not", below).

Since 2026-09-29 the same rule reaches one stage earlier, extending Pavol's rule on rerunning the gate after a repair that only added tests (`reviews/batch-6.5b-review.md`, finding 2 and measure 2). When the gate that ran beside the review is red, and the review's corrections left it standing, the review's repair is given the gate's failing lines (`repairTestsStep`) and returns `failingAnswered` f
or them, answering a line only where its own ruling's edit is to the test the line names and its harness run passes it. After a repair of tests and records only, `repairRerun` is asked again with that gate; when every line is answered and no suite count fell, no gate judge and no gate repair run, and the commit records the runs and the answered lines beside the gate's summary, as after a gate's re
pair. A line the repair does not answer, a repair that changed code, and a review whose judge rules land take the paths they took before. The reason: in climb batch 6.5b the review's repair had fixed the one `.test` line the gate was red on and run the pair through the harness, placed and on the fix, yet the script passed its runs as answering nothing (`answered: []`) and sent the red gate to a ga
te judge, which ruled that the repair was already on `main`, and to a gate repair that ran the same pair again: 42 minutes, 30 of them the judge's wait on a permission prompt, and 0.38M tokens with a top-tier ruling.

